hadiths[2]{hadithnumber,text}:
"1569","Ahmad bin Al-Miqdam told us, Malazem bin Amr told us, my grandfather Abdullah bin Badr told me, on the authority of Abdul Rahman bin Ali bin Shayban, On the authority of his father, Ali bin Shayban, who was one of the delegation, he said: We prayed behind him, meaning the Prophet, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, and the Prophet of God, may God’s prayers and peace be upon him, decided. He said the prayer, and he saw an individual man praying behind the row, so the Prophet of God, may God bless him and grant him peace, stood over him until he finished his prayer, then said to him: “Receive... Your prayer, there is no prayer for an individual behind the row.”"
"1570","Abu Bakr said: And in the reports of Wabisa bin Ma`abad: He saw a man praying behind the row alone, so he ordered him to repeat the prayer. Some of our companions, and some of those who adhere to the Iraqi school of thought, protested against the permissibility of the prayer of the person being led behind the row alone, as it is very similar to This issue, they used as evidence the story of Anas bin Malik, “that he prayed with a woman behind the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, and he placed her on his right. And the woman is behind that.” They said: If it is permissible for a woman to stand behind the row alone, then it is permissible for the worshiper to pray behind the row alone! In my opinion, this argument is wrong. Because the Sunnah for a woman is to stand behind the line alone if there is no other woman with her, and it is not permissible for her to stand in the imam’s shoes. Nor in a row with the men. If the men who are being led in congregation are one, then his Sunnah is for him to stand to the right of his imam, and if they are a group, then they stand in a row behind him. The imam, until he completes the first row, and it is not permissible for the man to stand behind the imam while the person being led is one, and there is no disagreement among the people of knowledge that this If the action is done by an actor, and he stands behind an imam, and the person being followed stands to his right, contrary to the Sunnah of the Prophet, may God bless him and grant him peace, even though they differed regarding The obligation to repeat the prayer, and if the woman stands behind the row and there is no other woman with her or any women, she should do what she was told to do, and what is her Sunnah in Standing up. And if a man stands in the row alone, he does something that is not part of his Sunnah, since his Sunnah is to enter the row and line up with the people who are being led along with those who are praying behind him. So how can it be? What the person being led in prayer is rebuked from doing, which is contrary to his Sunnah in standing, is likened to the action of a woman who did what she was commanded to do, which is contrary to her Sunnah in standing. Behind the class alone? Thus, the thing that is similar to the one that is forbidden to the one that is commanded is negligent and negligent. It is a comparison between two opposite actions, since it is a similarity that is terminated. He is commanded to do so. So consider this word, and the proof for what we have mentioned will be clear to you, with the help of our Creator. Our Iraqi opponents on this issue claimed that if the woman stood in line with the men where the man was ordered to stand, she would have committed corruption. The prayer of the one on her right, and the one on her left, and the one praying behind her, and the man is commanded by them to stand in line with the men, so how is he likened? The act of a woman who, if she did it, would spoil the prayer of three of the worshipers, by the action of who is he commanded to do? If he does it, his action will not spoil anyone’s prayer."
